**No-go on the proposed re-ruling as written.** The date split is worth investigating first, but its cause is not yet established, and the proposed sentence could settle more than D414 decides.

The clearest counterexample is the fresh Poolside question. The Codex third readers did not simply say “the note disagrees with code”: they said it was an **already undertaken obligation**, which the existing brief says is due at its next annual filing. D414 says code sets an *estimated yearly figure’s* day, while keeping a day stated in the topic or article. How those rules apply together to this question needs to be resolved against its topic, article and full choices before all six marks are treated as the same error. [Codex rulings](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-fresh-a2-2026-11-16/choices/reading/third-reader/rulings-codex-1.jsonl>), [reader brief](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:98>), [D414](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4568>)

The proposed sentence is faithful for a yearly figure whose publication day is **an estimate**. As written, however, it could be read to make `expected_publication` conclusive even when the topic or article states a day, or when the separate obligation rule applies. It could also affect other marks that use the fiscal-year note, such as N’s reading of “this year” and “next year”; that needs a check, not an assumption. [Reader brief](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:106>)

The census establishes a striking **association**: 133 Codex first-reader failures and none from Claude on 225 flagged items. Its selector finds company figures whose shown month differs from the note; it does not establish that each day was correctly set, that no stated date applied, or that the note caused each failure. Those are readings, including repeated attempts, not 225 independent questions. [Census](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-split-census-output.txt>), [selector](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d_split_census.py>)

I would do a **$0 source-backed diagnostic first**: classify the disputed points by estimated annual figure, explicitly stated date and binding obligation; have both families independently read a blinded sample containing those cases plus controls where D should hold and fail. Show the unchanged reading beside the clarified reading. Re-ruling only previously disputed points cannot measure false passes or establish a general reader error rate, and a one-third threshold on Codex alone cannot prove the note was—or was not—the cause.

The postponement class should follow its stored census; it is a product fault, but the present fresh reading supplies one agreed send, so it does not yet displace this cheap check. Preserve the stopped fresh-comparison verdict and A2’s unaccepted status.

CONSENSUS: no, because the proposed clarification may override the obligation or stated-date rules, and the selected re-rulings cannot establish the cause or error rate of the split.
